    :index/constituents-source-note "Constituent candidates were selected from the agent's general knowledge of well-known, large-cap S&P 500 member companies (not from a freshly fetched/scraped official S&P Dow Jones Indices constituent list this session) -- honestly disclosed per ADR-2607199960 rather than citing a fabricated source URL for the list itself. Each individual company's LEI and ToS/legal-document text, by contrast, IS a real tool-verified fetch (GLEIF API + WebFetch/curl), recorded per-company below with real source URLs."
    :index/note "First seed pass, 2026-07-19: 8 candidates attempted, chosen to diversify away from the oil/pipeline/utility/airline/retail/chemicals/semiconductor/aerospace/waste sectors already heavily sampled by the ISIC track -- banking (JPMorgan Chase), CPG (Procter & Gamble, Coca-Cola), healthcare (Johnson & Johnson, UnitedHealth Group), payments (Visa), industrials (3M), and tech (Microsoft). 6/8 succeeded. JPMorgan Chase & Co. and Johnson & Johnson both had real GLEIF-verified LEIs but failed ToS fetch (bot-blocked/JS-shell) after 2 URL attempts each. All 6 successes used a curl+plain-text-extraction fallback after WebFetch alone 403'd/404'd/timed-out/summarized-instead-of-quoting on the same URLs for 5 of the 6 (only Visa needed just the redirect-follow). Iteration 2, same day: 6 more candidates attempted -- banking (Goldman Sachs), pharma (Pfizer, Merck), payments/tech (Mastercard, Adobe), industrials (Caterpillar). 3/6 succeeded (Goldman Sachs and Merck fetched directly via WebFetch on the first URL attempt with no retry needed; Pfizer needed the curl+footer-scan fallback after its first guessed URL 404'd). Adobe and Caterpillar were both fully bot-blocked at the network layer (Adobe: HTTP/2 stream reset + later HTTP 000 connection failure on both WebFetch and curl attempts against multiple hostnames; Caterpillar: HTTP 403 on every attempted hostname/path including the plain homepage) -- not just a wrong URL guess. Mastercard was HTTP 403 via both WebFetch and curl on its known terms-of-use path and its homepage; no substitute privacy-notice document was found on this pass (unlike Visa's precedent) so it was recorded as a clean tos-fetch-failed rather than stretched into a doc-type substitution. Bank of America Corporation (LEI 9DJT3UXIJIZJI4WXO774) and Wells Fargo & Company (LEI PBLD0EJDB5FWOLXP3B76) were both GLEIF-verified as real during this iteration's candidate research but turned out to already be recorded (with :tos-fetch-failed status) under a different progress-file row from earlier catalog work -- correctly not re-added here to avoid a duplicate LEI entry across files; see :progress/resume-from for detail. Iteration 3, same day: 8 more candidates attempted -- pharma (Eli Lilly, AbbVie), tech (Salesforce, Netflix, Meta Platforms), consumer/CPG (Colgate-Palmolive, Kimberly-Clark, Mondelez International). Only 2/8 succeeded. Salesforce's real Terms of Service page (found via a homepage footer-link scan after the legal-hub landing page turned out to be an index of links, not the ToS text itself) and Netflix's Terms of Use both fetched cleanly via curl on the first correctly-identified URL. Eli Lilly was blocked by a Cloudflare 'Just a moment...' JS challenge on every one of 3 URLs tried (lilly.com/terms-of-use redirect target, and a homepage-footer-scanned lillyhub.com copyright page). AbbVie and Kimberly-Clark were both HTTP 403 via curl AND WebFetch (2 clean attempts each, homepage returned only a tiny bot-check stub). Colgate-Palmolive's guessed corporate domain (colgatepalmolive.com) turned out to be a parked/for-sale domain (redirects to a GoDaddy sale page) -- retried on the real brand domain colgate.com, which was also HTTP 403 on 2 URL attempts. Meta Platforms was HTTP 400 on every one of 4 guessed Facebook/Meta legal URLs (facebook.com/legal/terms, about.meta.com/actions/legal-obligations, facebook.com/policies_center, mbasic.facebook.com/legal/terms) -- Meta aggressively blocks non-browser HTTP clients at the edge. Mondelez International's terms-of-use page returned HTTP 200 via curl but is a heavy client-side-rendered SPA (confirmed no embedded JSON/script payload containing the real legal text in the raw HTML); WebFetch on the same URL also explicitly reported it could only see nav/footer markup, not the substantive Terms of Use body -- recorded as a genuine fetch failure, not a wrong-URL guess."
